import ContentLayout from "../layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
---

<ContentLayout
  title="How Save Scum Automatically Backs Up Game Saves"
  heading="Pick the save folder. Keep the story."
  description="Set up Save Scum, confirm your first game save snapshot, let the desktop watcher preserve changes, and safely restore an earlier captured state."
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: "How it works", href: "/how-it-works/" }]}
  eyebrow="FROM SAVE FILE TO SECOND CHANCE"
>
  <p>
    Save Scum works with ordinary local files. It does not need a Steam account
    or a game-specific plugin. You choose the folder, it keeps snapshots, and
    you decide which moment to restore. Check{" "}
    <a href="/download/">current platform and release status</a> first.
  </p>
  <h2>01 — Find where the game actually saves</h2>
  <p>
    Choose <strong>Add game</strong> in the desktop app. Detect a known location
    or select the save directory yourself. Installed-game detection and
    save-folder discovery are different: finding an installed game does not
    prove where its saves live.
  </p>
  <p>
    Make a normal in-game save, then confirm the files in the selected folder
    changed. Select the directory that contains the current progress, not a new
    folder you created for backups. Save Scum stores snapshots separately. Empty
    folders cannot provide a usable starting point.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href="/games/">
      See save-location guidance and the bundled discovery definitions.
    </a>
  </p>
  <h2>02 — Inspect the first snapshot</h2>
  <p>
    The desktop app captures an initial state when you register a usable folder.
    Open its history and check the file count, path, and captured contents. A
    successful-looking backup of the wrong directory is still the wrong backup.
  </p>
  <p>
    Create a named checkpoint before your next experiment. Keep an independent
    copy of important saves while getting familiar with the restore workflow.
  </p>
  <h2>03 — Keep playing while the watcher keeps history</h2>
  <p>
    Leave Save Scum running with monitoring enabled. After the game writes files
    and activity settles, the watcher groups changes into a snapshot. It skips
    unchanged automatic captures and checks file consistency before committing.
  </p>
  <p>
    Pause monitoring when you need to troubleshoot a folder. If the directory
    becomes unavailable, storage fills up, or capture fails, investigate the
    reported state rather than assuming every moment was recorded. The app
    cannot capture unsaved memory or server-only progress.
  </p>
  <h2>04 — Restore a moment, carefully</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Exit the game and confirm it is no longer writing saves.</li>
    <li>
      Open the game’s history. Select a healthy snapshot from before the
      unwanted change.
    </li>
    <li>Review the destination path and added, changed, and removed files.</li>
    <li>
      Confirm the restore. Save Scum preserves the current files in a safety
      checkpoint before replacing them.
    </li>
    <li>
      Review the result and recovery path. Open the game and verify the expected
      progress.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    If the checkpoint matches the current files, the app reports that no changes
    are needed. Checkpoints from an old, corrected folder cannot silently
    overwrite a different save location.
  </p>
  <h2>05 — Account for cloud sync and mods</h2>
  <p>
    A restored local save may differ from a cloud copy. Follow the game or
    platform’s sync guidance; do not choose a conflict resolution blindly. A
    snapshot also does not restore the game executable or your entire mod setup.
    Keep track of the game and mod versions a save needs.
  </p>
  <p>
    Read{" "}
    <a href="/guides/how-to-back-up-steam-saves/">the Steam backup guide</a> for
    sync considerations, or{" "}
    <a href="/guides/how-to-back-up-game-saves/">the general backup guide</a> to
    build a wider backup routine.
  </p>
</ContentLayout>
